#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Guard: timers on the input path are give-up nets, nothing else (owner rule, 2026-11-08).

THE RULE. A timer on the key path may only be a GIVE-UP NET: the last resort for an event that never
came. A net hands text back to the editor, keeps it held, shows a notice, or (owner ruling
2026-11-09, #1996, kept 2026-11-10) WRITES THE HELD KEYS TO THE PTY IN TYPED ORDER: a hold for the
shell that gives up sends what a plain terminal would have sent all along, and a later line never
runs without the line typed before it. A net NEVER discards, reorders, merges or splits a key, never
chooses between the shell and the agent, never submits an editor line that was not already certain to
be a shell line, and never re-asks. Decisions come from EVENTS (the shell announced itself, the
program owns the tty, the block ended); a timer only stops a missing event from losing keys. The
2026-11-08 wording ("a timer never writes to the pty") was superseded by #1996; DECLINED.md has the
decision.

WHAT THIS CHECKS. It scans the input-path files in INPUT_PATH_FILES below and finds timers:
  Rust   `Timer::after` / `Timer::interval` / `spawn_after` / `tokio::time::sleep|timeout` /
         `thread::sleep` call sites, and Duration constants named like *_GIVE_UP, *_DELAY,
         *_WINDOW, *_QUIET, *_SETTLE, *_BUDGET, *_RESET, *_NET, *_CEILING, *_TIMEOUT, *_GRACE,
         *_PATIENCE, *_HOLD, *_CAP, *_BACKSTOP, *_WAIT, *_PACING, *_POLL, *_RECHECK, *_NOTICE, *_AFTER,
         (each as a whole underscore-delimited token anywhere in the name)
         (or any Duration constant a timer call site uses).
  shell  `read -t`, `sleep N`, `zselect -t`, $SECONDS / $EPOCHREALTIME arithmetic, TMOUT,
         pwsh `Start-Sleep` / `Wait-Event -Timeout`.
Test files (*_test.rs, *_tests.rs, tests/ dirs) are exempt.

EACH timer must (1) sit within 5 lines of a comment `give-up-net: <what it hands back / keeps held / writes>`
(Rust `//`, shell `#`), and (2) have its identifier in script/input_path_timers.allow
(`path<TAB>identifier<TAB>what it does`). The identifier is the constant's name; for a call site
with no constant (`Timer::after(delay)`, `sleep 5`) it is the enclosing function's name. A use of a
constant is covered by the constant's own definition. Allowlist entries that no longer match code
are STALE and fail too (delete the entry when the timer goes).

PENDING-CONVERSION. The allowlist's last section holds timers known to break the rule that are being
converted to events. The default run prints a WARNING per entry; `--strict` (release tier) FAILS on
any. Moving an entry into the compliant section needs the code to really be a give-up net.

PACING (owner-exempt 2026-11-09). A third allowlist section, `# PACING`, holds timers that pace OUR OWN
bootstrap bytes into a user-space pty relay, a container proxy or a cooked tty whose consumption is
unobservable (#960, #1384). They never carry user keys. They need a `pacing: <why>` comment (instead of
`give-up-net:`), they are not pending under `--strict`, and the guard FAILS if the code path of a pacing
timer can carry user keystrokes. How that is checked: for every pacing entry the guard takes each Rust
function that references the pacing constant (or is the pacing function), plus the other pacing
functions of the file, strips comments and strings, and fails when the body mentions a user-key API
(write_user_bytes*, user_bytes*, UserInput*, user_input*, held_keys*, release_held_keys, typeahead*,
PtyIntent). The pacing path may only move bytes the app itself built. This is a name-based check: a new
user-key entry point with a different name must be added to USER_KEY_RE.

SCOPE RULE (widened 2026-11-09). INPUT_PATH_FILES are scanned in full. INPUT_PATH_LISTED_FILES are
mostly-non-input files (UI, AI, network, rendering) that hold a few timers touching where a key, paste,
Enter or pty write goes, or deciding input state: only the identifiers listed in the allowlist for that
file are enforced (comment, allowlist, stale check); their other timers are ignored as noise. A timer
that never touches input (animation, telemetry, network retry, shutdown, rendering) belongs in neither
list. A new input-relevant timer in a listed file is NOT detected until its identifier is added, so
add the file to INPUT_PATH_FILES when it grows several.

OTHER OWNER-EXEMPT CLASSES (2026-11-09), each its own allowlist section, its own required comment tag
(instead of give-up-net:), and not counted by --strict:
  # UI-CONFIRM   `ui-confirm:`   double-press confirmation windows and question auto-advance; no keys at risk.
  # SAMPLING     `sampling:`     timers that only schedule a probe sample; the probe result or an event
                               decides, never elapsed time alone (a timer that decides alone is PENDING).
  # REPLY-PACING `reply-pacing:` our own terminal-query replies, never user keys; like PACING, the guard fails
                               if its code path mentions a user-key API.

ADDING A TIMER. Prefer an event. If a give-up net is truly needed: add the constant (or call), put
`// give-up-net: <what it hands back / keeps held>` next to it, and add one line to
script/input_path_timers.allow. If you cannot honestly write that comment, the timer is not allowed.
Adding a file: append it to INPUT_PATH_FILES.

Usage: script/check_input_path_timers [--strict] | --self-test
Exit 0: clean. Exit 1: offending timers, stale entries (or pending entries with --strict).
"""
import os
import re
import sys
import tempfile
from pathlib import Path

# The single list of input-path files. Entries are files, directories (scanned recursively) or globs,
# relative to the repo root. Extend here.
INPUT_PATH_FILES = [
    "app/src/terminal/writeable_pty",
    "app/src/terminal/view/paste_queue.rs",
    "app/src/terminal/view/subshell_ready.rs",
    "app/src/terminal/model/typeahead_handoff.rs",
    "app/src/terminal/line_editor_status.rs",
    "app/src/terminal/input.rs",
    "app/src/terminal/view.rs",
    "app/src/terminal/model/block/interaction_mode.rs",
    # the hand-over machinery the files above delegate to
    "app/src/terminal/view/ssh_edges.rs",
    "app/src/terminal/view/ssh_typeahead.rs",
    "app/src/terminal/view/ssh_queue.rs",
    "app/src/terminal/view/route_facts.rs",
    "app/src/terminal/input/enter_queue.rs",
    "app/src/terminal/input/paste_queue.rs",
    "app/src/terminal/input/native_completions.rs",
    "app/src/terminal/input_queue",
    # bundled shell bootstrap / integration scripts (the in-band completion exchange lives here)
    "app/assets/bundled/bootstrap",
    "app/assets/bundled/ssh",
]
# Files that are MOSTLY NOT input (UI, network, AI, rendering) but hold one or more timers that touch
# where a key, paste, Enter or pty write goes, or decide input state. They are scanned like the files
# above, but only the identifiers LISTED in script/input_path_timers.allow for that file are enforced
# (comment + allowlist, and stale detection); other timers in these files are ignored as noise.
# Scope rule: a whole file goes in INPUT_PATH_FILES when most of its timers are input-relevant; a file
# goes here when only a few are. Anything that never touches input stays out of both lists.
INPUT_PATH_LISTED_FILES = [
    "app/src/search/ai_context_menu/view.rs",
    "app/src/terminal/model/block.rs",
    "app/src/terminal/model/session.rs",
    "app/src/terminal/local_tty/terminal_manager.rs",
    "app/src/terminal/local_tty/terminal_attributes.rs",
    "app/src/terminal/local_tty/event_loop.rs",
    "app/src/terminal/cli_agent_sessions/mod.rs",
    "app/src/ai/blocklist/controller.rs",
    "app/src/ai/blocklist/input_model.rs",
    "app/src/ai/blocklist/agent_view/controller.rs",
    "app/src/ai/blocklist/inline_action/ask_user_question_view.rs",
    "app/src/ai/blocklist/action_model/execute/shell_command.rs",
    "crates/warpui_core/src/runtime/terminal_probe.rs",
    "crates/warpui/src/windowing/winit/linux/clipboard.rs",
    "crates/warp_tui/src/terminal_background.rs",
    "crates/warp_tui/src/terminal_session_view.rs",
    "crates/warp_tui/src/terminal_session_view/input_detection.rs",
    "crates/warp_tui/src/tui_ask_question_view.rs",
]
ALLOW_FILE = "script/input_path_timers.allow"
PENDING_MARK = "# PENDING-CONVERSION"
PACING_MARK = "# PACING"
# Owner-exempt labelled classes: section marker -> the comment tag its entries need instead of give-up-net.
EXEMPT_SECTIONS = {
    "# PACING": "pacing",
    "# UI-CONFIRM": "ui-confirm",
    "# SAMPLING": "sampling",
    "# REPLY-PACING": "reply-pacing",
}
USER_KEY_RE = re.compile(
    r"\b(?:write_user_bytes\w*|user_bytes\w*|UserInput\w*|user_input\w*|held_keys\w*|"
    r"release_held_keys|typeahead\w*|PtyIntent)\b"
)
NOTE_RE = re.compile(r"give-up-net:\s*\S")
WINDOW = 5

# A whole underscore-delimited token anywhere in the name (so THRESHOLD never matches HOLD).
NAME_RE = re.compile(
    r"(?:^|_)(?:GIVE_UP|DELAY|WINDOW|QUIET|SETTLE|BUDGET|RESET|NET|CEILING|TIMEOUT|GRACE|PATIENCE|HOLD|"
    r"CAP|BACKSTOP|WAIT|PACING|POLL|RECHECK|NOTICE|AFTER|DEBOUNCE|DEADLINE|DRAIN|DISMISS)(?:_|$)"
)
CONST_RE = re.compile(r"\b(?:const|static\s+ref|static)\s+([A-Z][A-Z0-9_]+)\s*:")
DUR_RE = re.compile(r"Duration")
DURFROM_RE = re.compile(r"from_(?:millis|secs|micros|nanos|secs_f32|secs_f64)")
CALL_RE = re.compile(
    r"(?:\bTimer::(?:after|interval)|\bspawn_after|\btokio::time::(?:sleep|timeout)|\bthread::sleep)\s*\("
)
FN_RE = re.compile(r"^\s*(?:pub(?:\([^)]*\))?\s+)?(?:async\s+)?(?:unsafe\s+)?fn\s+(\w+)")
UPPER_RE = re.compile(r"\b([A-Z][A-Z0-9]*_[A-Z0-9_]+|[A-Z]{4,})\b")

SHELL_FN_RES = [
    re.compile(r"^\s*function\s+([\w-]+)"),
    re.compile(r"^\s*([A-Za-z_][\w-]*)\s*\(\)\s*\{?"),
    re.compile(r"^\s*([A-Za-z_][\w-]*)\s+\(\)\s*\{?"),
]
SHELL_PATTERNS = [
    re.compile(r"\bread\b[^#\n]*\s-[A-Za-z]*t[A-Za-z]*\s"),
    re.compile(r"(?:^|[\s;(&|])sleep\s+[0-9$]"),
    re.compile(r"\bzselect\s+-t\b"),
    re.compile(r"\(\(.*\b(?:SECONDS|EPOCHREALTIME)\b"),
    re.compile(r"\bTMOUT\b"),
    re.compile(r"\bStart-Sleep\b"),
    re.compile(r"\bWait-Event\b.*-Timeout"),
]
SHELL_SUFFIXES = (".sh", ".ps1", ".nu", ".fish", ".zsh", ".bash")


def is_test_file(rel):
    name = os.path.basename(rel)
    parts = rel.split("/")
    return name.endswith(("_test.rs", "_tests.rs")) or name in ("tests.rs",) or "tests" in parts[:-1]


def expand(root, entries):
    out = []
    for e in entries:
        p = root / e
        if p.is_dir():
            for q in sorted(p.rglob("*")):
                if q.is_file():
                    out.append(str(q.relative_to(root)))
        elif p.is_file():
            out.append(e)
        else:
            for q in sorted(root.glob(e)):
                if q.is_file():
                    out.append(str(q.relative_to(root)))
    seen, res = set(), []
    for f in out:
        if f in seen or is_test_file(f):
            continue
        if not (f.endswith(".rs") or f.endswith(SHELL_SUFFIXES)):
            continue
        seen.add(f)
        res.append(f)
    return res


def comment_near(lines, idx, prefix, tag="give-up-net"):
    note = NOTE_RE if tag == "give-up-net" else re.compile(r"(?<![\w-])" + re.escape(tag) + r":\s*\S")
    lo, hi = max(0, idx - WINDOW), min(len(lines), idx + WINDOW + 1)
    for j in range(lo, hi):
        s = lines[j].lstrip()
        if s.startswith(prefix) and note.search(s):
            return True
        # a trailing comment on a code line counts too
        k = lines[j].find(prefix + " " + tag + ":")
        if k >= 0 and note.search(lines[j][k:]):
            return True
    return False


def call_arg(lines, idx, m):
    """Text of the call's argument list (balanced parens, may span lines)."""
    text = "\n".join(lines[idx:idx + 8])
    s = text[m.end():]
    depth, buf = 1, []
    for ch in s:
        if ch == "(":
            depth += 1
        elif ch == ")":
            depth -= 1
            if depth == 0:
                break
        buf.append(ch)
    return "".join(buf)


def scan_rust(rel, lines):
    """Returns (consts{name: line}, sites[(line, ident_or_None, enclosing_fn)])."""
    consts, sites = {}, []
    cur_fn = ""
    for i, line in enumerate(lines):
        if line.lstrip().startswith("//"):
            continue
        mf = FN_RE.match(line)
        if mf:
            cur_fn = mf.group(1)
        mc = CONST_RE.search(line)
        if mc:
            chunk = "\n".join(lines[i:i + 4])
            chunk = chunk.split(";")[0]
            if DUR_RE.search(chunk) and DURFROM_RE.search(chunk):
                consts.setdefault(mc.group(1), i)
        mcall = CALL_RE.search(line)
        if mcall:
            arg = call_arg(lines, i, mcall)
            mu = UPPER_RE.search(arg)
            sites.append((i, mu.group(1) if mu else None, cur_fn))
    return consts, sites


def scan_shell(rel, lines):
    sites, cur_fn = [], ""
    for i, line in enumerate(lines):
        if line.lstrip().startswith("#"):
            continue
        for r in SHELL_FN_RES:
            m = r.match(line)
            if m and m.group(1) not in ("if", "while", "for", "case", "function"):
                cur_fn = m.group(1)
                break
        if any(p.search(line) for p in SHELL_PATTERNS):
            sites.append((i, cur_fn or "<top>"))
    return sites


def parse_allow(text):
    ok, pending, pacing = {}, {}, {}
    exempt_tags = {}  # key -> tag, for every owner-exempt class entry
    cur = ok
    cur_tag = None
    for n, raw in enumerate(text.splitlines(), 1):
        if raw.startswith(PENDING_MARK):
            cur, cur_tag = pending, None
            continue
        hit = next((m for m in EXEMPT_SECTIONS if raw.startswith(m)), None)
        if hit:
            cur, cur_tag = pacing, EXEMPT_SECTIONS[hit]
            continue
        if not raw.strip() or raw.lstrip().startswith("#"):
            continue
        parts = raw.split("\t")
        if len(parts) < 3 or not all(p.strip() for p in parts[:3]):
            raise SystemExit(f"{ALLOW_FILE}:{n}: want path<TAB>identifier<TAB>what it does")
        cur[(parts[0].strip(), parts[1].strip())] = (n, parts[2].strip())
        if cur_tag:
            exempt_tags[(parts[0].strip(), parts[1].strip())] = cur_tag
    return ok, pending, pacing, exempt_tags


def strip_code(text):
    text = re.sub(r"//[^\n]*", "", text)
    return re.sub(r'"(?:\\.|[^"\\])*"', '""', text)


def fn_bodies(lines):
    """{fn name: body text} for every fn in a Rust file (brace-balanced, comments and strings stripped)."""
    out = {}
    i = 0
    while i < len(lines):
        m = FN_RE.match(lines[i])
        if not m:
            i += 1
            continue
        depth, started, j, buf = 0, False, i, []
        while j < len(lines):
            code = strip_code(lines[j])
            buf.append(code)
            depth += code.count("{") - code.count("}")
            if "{" in code:
                started = True
            if started and depth <= 0:
                break
            if not started and code.rstrip().endswith(";"):
                break
            j += 1
        out.setdefault(m.group(1), "\n".join(buf))
        i = i + 1
    return out


def pacing_user_key_errors(root, allow_pacing):
    errs = []
    by_file = {}
    for (rel, ident), (n, _) in allow_pacing.items():
        by_file.setdefault(rel, []).append((ident, n))
    for rel, idents in by_file.items():
        if not rel.endswith(".rs"):
            errs.append(f"{ALLOW_FILE}: PACING entries must be Rust timers; {rel} is not")
            continue
        bodies = fn_bodies((root / rel).read_text(errors="replace").split("\n"))
        names = {i for i, _ in idents}
        checked = set()
        for fn, body in bodies.items():
            refs = any(re.search(r"\b" + re.escape(i) + r"\b", body) for i in names)
            if fn in names or refs or (fn == "start_paced_writes" and refs):
                checked.add(fn)
        for fn in sorted(checked):
            hit = USER_KEY_RE.search(bodies[fn])
            if hit:
                errs.append(f"{rel}: PACING path `{fn}` mentions user-key API `{hit.group(0)}`; a pacing timer must only carry bytes the app built (move it to the give-up-net or PENDING section)")
    return errs


def run(root, files, allow_text, strict, listed=()):
    allow_ok, allow_pending, allow_pacing, exempt_tags = parse_allow(allow_text)
    allowed = {**allow_ok, **allow_pending, **allow_pacing}
    errors, warnings = [], []
    missing_note = []  # (key, message): waived for PENDING entries, which are not nets
    found = set()
    all_consts = {}   # name -> (file, line)
    per_file = {}
    for rel in files:
        lines = (root / rel).read_text(errors="replace").split("\n")
        per_file[rel] = lines
        if rel.endswith(".rs"):
            consts, sites = scan_rust(rel, lines)
            for c, ln in consts.items():
                all_consts.setdefault(c, (rel, ln))
            per_file[rel] = (lines, consts, sites)
        else:
            per_file[rel] = (lines, None, scan_shell(rel, lines))
    used = set()
    for rel, (lines, consts, sites) in per_file.items():
        if consts is None:
            for ln, fn in sites:
                key = (rel, fn)
                found.add(key)
                tag = exempt_tags.get(key, "give-up-net")
                if not comment_near(lines, ln, "#", tag):
                    missing_note.append((key, f"{rel}:{ln + 1}: shell timer in `{fn}` has no `# {tag}: <why>` comment within {WINDOW} lines"))
            continue
        for ln, ident, fn in sites:
            if ident and ident in all_consts:
                used.add(ident)
                continue  # covered by the constant's definition
            key = (rel, ident or fn)
            found.add(key)
            tag = exempt_tags.get(key, "give-up-net")
            if not comment_near(lines, ln, "//", tag):
                missing_note.append((key, f"{rel}:{ln + 1}: timer call in `{fn}` has no `// {tag}: <why>` comment within {WINDOW} lines"))
    for name, (rel, ln) in sorted(all_consts.items()):
        if not (NAME_RE.search(name) or name in used):
            continue
        found.add((rel, name))
        tag = exempt_tags.get((rel, name), "give-up-net")
        if not comment_near(per_file[rel][0], ln, "//", tag):
            missing_note.append(((rel, name), f"{rel}:{ln + 1}: timer constant {name} has no `// {tag}: <why>` comment within {WINDOW} lines"))
    errors.extend(pacing_user_key_errors(root, {k: v for k, v in allow_pacing.items() if exempt_tags.get(k) in ("pacing", "reply-pacing")}))
    # Listed (mostly-non-input) files: only identifiers named in the allowlist are enforced.
    found = {k for k in found if k[0] not in listed or k in allowed}
    missing_note = [(k, m) for k, m in missing_note if k[0] not in listed or k in allowed]
    errors.extend(m for k, m in missing_note if k not in allow_pending)
    for key in sorted(found):
        if key not in allowed:
            errors.append(f"{key[0]}: timer `{key[1]}` is not in {ALLOW_FILE} (add `path<TAB>identifier<TAB>what it does` only if it is a give-up net)")
    for key, (n, _) in sorted(allowed.items()):
        if key not in found:
            errors.append(f"{ALLOW_FILE}:{n}: STALE entry {key[0]} {key[1]} matches no timer in the code; delete it")
    for key, (n, what) in sorted(allow_pending.items()):
        msg = f"{ALLOW_FILE}:{n}: PENDING-CONVERSION {key[0]} {key[1]}: {what}"
        (errors if strict else warnings).append(msg)
    return errors, warnings


RULE = (
    "Rule: a timer on the input path may only be a give-up net: it hands text back to the editor, keeps it\n"
    "held, or writes the held keys to the pty in typed order (owner ruling 2026-11-09, #1996). It never\n"
    "discards or reorders a key, never chooses shell or agent, never submits an undecided line, never\n"
    "re-asks. Annotate it with `give-up-net: <what it hands back / keeps held / writes>` and add it to\n"
    "script/input_path_timers.allow."
)


def self_test():
    ok = True
    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as d:
        root = Path(d)
        (root / "f.rs").write_text(
            "const A_GIVE_UP: Duration = Duration::from_secs(3);\n"
            "fn go() {\n    ctx.spawn(Timer::after(A_GIVE_UP), |m| {});\n}\n"
        )
        (root / "g.rs").write_text(
            "fn go2() {\n    // give-up-net: hands the text back\n    ctx.spawn(Timer::after(delay), |m| {});\n}\n"
        )
        (root / "s.sh").write_text("f () {\n  # give-up-net: hands the byte back\n  read -t 0.1 x\n}\n")
        files = ["f.rs", "g.rs", "s.sh"]
        good_allow = "g.rs\tgo2\tx\ns.sh\tf\ty\n"

        def expect(label, allow, needle, strict=False, fs=files, want_fail=True):
            nonlocal ok
            errs, _ = run(root, fs, allow, strict)
            hit = any(needle in e for e in errs)
            if want_fail != bool(errs) or (needle and want_fail and not hit):
                print(f"self-test FAIL: {label}: errors={errs}")
                ok = False
            else:
                print(f"self-test ok: {label}")

        # unannotated timer (constant without a comment) is caught, even though it is allowlisted
        expect("unannotated timer", good_allow + "f.rs\tA_GIVE_UP\tz\n", "A_GIVE_UP has no")
        # annotated but missing from the allowlist
        (root / "f.rs").write_text(
            "// give-up-net: hands the line back\nconst A_GIVE_UP: Duration = Duration::from_secs(3);\n"
        )
        expect("missing allowlist entry", good_allow, "not in script/input_path_timers.allow")
        # allowlisted + annotated is clean; a stale entry is caught
        expect("clean", good_allow + "f.rs\tA_GIVE_UP\tz\n", "", want_fail=False)
        expect("stale entry", good_allow + "f.rs\tA_GIVE_UP\tz\nf.rs\tGONE_NET\tz\n", "STALE")
        # pending: warning by default, failure with --strict
        pend = good_allow + "f.rs\tA_GIVE_UP\tz\n" + PENDING_MARK + "\ng.rs\tgo2\tz\n"
        pend = "s.sh\tf\ty\nf.rs\tA_GIVE_UP\tz\n" + PENDING_MARK + "\ng.rs\tgo2\tz\n"
        expect("pending warns", pend, "", want_fail=False)
        expect("pending fails under --strict", pend, "PENDING-CONVERSION", strict=True)
        # Listed (mostly-non-input) file: an unlisted timer is ignored, a listed stale one fails.
        (root / "l.rs").write_text("fn noisy() {\n    ctx.spawn(Timer::after(d), |m| {});\n}\n")
        rest, _ = run(root, ["l.rs"], "", False, {"l.rs"})
        if rest:
            print(f"self-test FAIL: listed file reported an unlisted timer: {rest}")
            ok = False
        else:
            print("self-test ok: listed file ignores unlisted timers")
        stale, _ = run(root, ["l.rs"], "l.rs\tgone\tz\n", False, {"l.rs"})
        if not any("STALE" in e for e in stale):
            print(f"self-test FAIL: listed stale entry not caught: {stale}")
            ok = False
        else:
            print("self-test ok: listed file stale entry caught")
        # The other exempt classes: each needs its OWN tag, none is pending under --strict.
        for sec, tag in (("# UI-CONFIRM", "ui-confirm"), ("# SAMPLING", "sampling"), ("# REPLY-PACING", "reply-pacing")):
            body = "const X_PACING: Duration = Duration::from_millis(5);\nfn go() {\n    ctx.spawn(Timer::after(X_PACING), |m| {});\n}\n"
            (root / "c.rs").write_text("// pacing: wrong tag\n" + body)
            expect(f"{tag} needs its own tag", sec + "\nc.rs\tX_PACING\tz\n", f"{tag}: <why>", fs=["c.rs"])
            (root / "c.rs").write_text(f"// {tag}: fine\n" + body)
            expect(f"{tag} with its tag is clean and not strict-pending", sec + "\nc.rs\tX_PACING\tz\n", "", fs=["c.rs"], strict=True, want_fail=False)
        # PACING rule: needs a `pacing:` comment (give-up-net does not do), is not pending under
        # --strict, and fails when its code path mentions a user-key API.
        PAC = PACING_MARK + "\n"
        (root / "p.rs").write_text(
            "// give-up-net: wrong tag\nconst X_PACING: Duration = Duration::from_millis(5);\n"
            "fn go() {\n    ctx.spawn(Timer::after(X_PACING), |m| {});\n}\n"
        )
        pal = "p.rs\tX_PACING\tz\n"
        expect("pacing needs a pacing: comment", PAC + pal, "pacing: <why>", fs=["p.rs"])
        (root / "p.rs").write_text(
            "// pacing: our own bootstrap chunks\nconst X_PACING: Duration = Duration::from_millis(5);\n"
            "fn go() {\n    ctx.spawn(Timer::after(X_PACING), |m| {});\n}\n"
        )
        expect("pacing with comment is clean", PAC + pal, "", fs=["p.rs"], want_fail=False)
        expect("pacing is not pending under --strict", PAC + pal, "", fs=["p.rs"], strict=True, want_fail=False)
        (root / "p.rs").write_text(
            "// pacing: our own bootstrap chunks\nconst X_PACING: Duration = Duration::from_millis(5);\n"
            "fn go(&mut self) {\n    self.write_user_bytes_to_pty(b, ctx);\n    ctx.spawn(Timer::after(X_PACING), |m| {});\n}\n"
        )
        expect("pacing path with a user-key API fails", PAC + pal, "user-key API", fs=["p.rs"])
    print("self-test: " + ("ok" if ok else "FAILED"))
    return 0 if ok else 1


def main():
    args = sys.argv[1:]
    if "--self-test" in args:
        return self_test()
    strict = "--strict" in args
    root = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent
    files = expand(root, INPUT_PATH_FILES + INPUT_PATH_LISTED_FILES)
    listed = set(expand(root, INPUT_PATH_LISTED_FILES)) - set(expand(root, INPUT_PATH_FILES))
    if not files:
        print("check_input_path_timers: no input-path files found (INPUT_PATH_FILES stale?)", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    allow_text = (root / ALLOW_FILE).read_text()
    errors, warnings = run(root, files, allow_text, strict, listed)
    for w in warnings:
        print("WARNING " + w, file=sys.stderr)
    if errors:
        for e in errors:
            print(e, file=sys.stderr)
        print("\ncheck_input_path_timers: FAILED\n" + RULE, file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    print(f"check_input_path_timers: ok ({len(files)} files, {len(warnings)} pending)")
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
